---
title: "The rendering pipeline"
---

Rendering is where `post.tmd` stops being text and becomes the **block list**
everything else keys off: its heading, its paragraph, and its code cell, each a
separate block with its own identity. The entry point is
`render_document_with_includes(src, base_dir)` in `render/mod.rs`; it runs a fixed
sequence of stages, each a small, testable function.

```{mermaid}
flowchart TB
  src[".tmd source"] --> inc["includes::resolve<br/>{{< include >}} + source map"]
  inc --> sc["expand_shortcodes<br/>extension {{< name >}} templates"]
  sc --> spans["scan_div_spans<br/>record ::: fenced divs"]
  spans --> pre["preprocess<br/>blank ::: markers (line-preserving)"]
  pre --> ast["comrak parse_document<br/>(+ sourcepos)"]
  ast --> walk["walk top-level nodes<br/>→ FlatBlock (emit HTML, hash id)"]
  walk --> group["group_divs<br/>rewrap into callouts/columns"]
  group --> cite["table captions + cite::process<br/>citations, @refs"]
  cite --> blocks["RenderedDoc { blocks, title, … }"]
```

## Stage by stage

1. **Include resolution** (`includes.rs`). `{{< include path >}}` shortcodes are
   expanded inline, and a `Vec<LineOrigin>` source map records, for every line of
   the expanded text, which file + line it came from. Cycles are detected and left
   in place. This is what makes click-to-source work *across files*.
2. **Shortcode expansion** (`render/extension/`). `{{< name args >}}` tags
   declared by the active format extension expand to their HTML templates. It is
   **line-preserving** (each tag opens and closes on one line, the template's
   newlines collapsed) so the source map from stage 1 stays valid.
3. **Fenced-div scan + preprocess** (`render/divs.rs`). The `:::` fenced divs aren't
   CommonMark, so their spans are recorded first, then the marker lines are blanked
   *without changing the line count* (again preserving sourcepos). The inner
   content then parses as ordinary blocks.
4. **Parse** (`comrak`, with `sourcepos` on). One AST for the whole document, with
   every node carrying its `startLine:col-endLine:col`. The parse and the walk that
   follows run on a worker thread with a **256 MB stack** (`render_internal` spawns a
   scoped thread, `stack_size(256 * 1024 * 1024)`). The reason is concrete: deeply
   nested Markdown (blockquotes, lists) drives deep recursion in both comrak's parser
   and Taliesin's block emission, and on the default ~8 MB thread stack that recursion
   overflows at around 3000 levels. A stack overflow is not a catchable panic, it
   **aborts the whole process**: one pathological document would crash a `build` or take
   down the live preview server outright. The big stack absorbs any realistic nesting;
   an ordinary panic inside the worker is propagated to the caller unchanged. (If
   spawning the big-stack worker fails, for instance under a strict `ulimit -v`, render
   falls back to the current default-stack thread rather than failing the render.)
5. **Walk → FlatBlock** (`render/mod.rs` + `render/emit.rs`). Each *top-level* node
   becomes one block: the emitter renders it to HTML (code highlighted by
   `highlight.rs`, math by `math.rs`), and the block gets a **content-hash id** and a
   `data-sourcepos` mapped back through the source map. A `` ```{lang} `` fence is
   recognised as an executable **cell** and recorded for the server to run later. The
   front-matter node is where metadata, the theme, and a format extension's includes
   are resolved.
6. **Regroup divs** (`render/divs.rs`). The flat blocks are wrapped back into
   callout / layout-grid / generic container blocks using the spans from stage 3.
   Inner blocks keep their own ids and sourcepos, so click-to-source works inside a
   callout.
7. **Captions + citations** (`apply_table_captions`, then `cite.rs`). A Pandoc table
   caption (`: caption {#tbl-x}` on the paragraph directly after a table) is **not** a
   sibling block in the output: `apply_table_captions` numbers it, folds it into the
   table as a `<caption>` first child, registers `tbl-x` for `@tbl-` references, and
   then **removes the caption paragraph block** so the caption lives as metadata *on*
   the table block. After that, `[@key]` citations and `@fig-`/`@sec-`/… cross-references
   are resolved against the bibliography and the cross-reference registry.

The result is a `RenderedDoc`: an ordered `Vec<Block>` plus the title, theme, TOC
flag, includes, and any non-fatal `warnings`.

## The block contract

Every emitted block carries three data attributes, and **the whole system depends
on them** (`crates/core/tests/corpus.rs` enforces it):

- `data-block-id`: a content hash. Because it's content-derived, an unchanged
  block keeps its id across edits and is never touched by the diff.
- `data-sourcepos`: the original source range (drives click-to-source).
- `data-source-file`: present only on blocks pulled in from an include.

### The id formula

`make_id` (`render/mod.rs`) computes the id from the block's **raw Markdown source**,
trimmed of surrounding whitespace, not from its rendered HTML. It hashes that text with
**64-bit FNV-1a** (`fnv1a`, the same small deterministic algorithm `freeze.rs` uses for
the execution cache, stable across runs and Taliesin versions), then formats the id as
`b-` followed by the **first 12 hex digits** of the 16-digit hash (e.g. `b-3af19c0e21d4`).

Two blocks with identical source text therefore hash to the same base id. To keep
`data-block-id` unique within a document (the diff's whole correctness rests on it),
`dedup_with_suffix` appends a positional `-N` to the second and later occurrences:
`b-…`, then `b-…-1`, `b-…-2`. So the first copy keeps the clean hash and only the
duplicates carry a tiebreak, which means inserting an *identical* paragraph above an
existing one renumbers only the duplicates, not every block below.

Hashing source text rather than position is the load-bearing choice: insert a paragraph
at the top of the document and every block below it keeps its id, so the diff sees one
insertion instead of a wholesale renumber, and an unchanged block is never repainted. A
generated block that has no source text of its own (the synthesized title header, the
References block, the footnotes section) skips the hash and uses a fixed id and an empty
`data-sourcepos`.

## Citations & cross-references

Citations and cross-references (`cite.rs`) are the last pipeline stage, and they
run as a **post-process over the already-rendered block HTML**: they transform only
plain-text runs, never the inside of a tag, code, or math span, so block sourcepos
is left untouched. `[@key]` citations become numbered links to a References block
appended to the document (the one structural change), formatted from the parsed
BibTeX. `@fig-` / `@sec-` / `@tbl-` / `@lst-` / `@eq-` references become links to their
anchor, carrying the resolved number when it is known. It is deliberately **pragmatic, not
a full CSL engine**. Cross-*page* references (a `@fig-` that lives in another
chapter) are resolved a layer up, in the site subsystem (see [The multi-page
subsystem](sites.tmd)).

Markdown footnotes (`[^name]`) split across **two separate emit paths**, and it is worth
being precise about which is which:

- A footnote **reference** (`[^name]` in the prose) is rendered *in place*, inline, as a
  superscript link (`<sup class="tali-fnref"><a href="#fn-name">N</a></sup>`) by the
  per-node emitter (`emit.rs`). It stays inside its surrounding block.
- A footnote **definition** (`[^name]: the note text`) is *not* rendered where it
  appears. The walk recognises comrak's footnote-definition nodes, collects them as
  `<li>`s in comrak's reference order, and after the citation pass appends them as one
  trailing `<section class="footnotes" role="doc-endnotes">` block (fixed id
  `tali-footnotes`, empty `data-sourcepos`) after any References block.

That trailing section flows through the block model and the diff exactly like the
References block: a generated, source-less block that the client treats no differently
from any other.

## Incremental updates

On a save the server re-runs the pipeline and calls `diff_blocks(old, new)`
(`diff.rs`): an LCS over the block-id sequences finds the stable anchors, and the
gaps between them become `Update` / `Insert` / `Remove` ops. Only those ops cross
the websocket, so the browser swaps a handful of elements while everything
else, including live canvases, stays put. (Code-cell *outputs* are blocks too,
keyed to the cell id, so re-running a cell updates only its output block.) Our
`tau` edit is the smallest case of all: only the cell's code changed, so the cell
re-runs and its output block re-renders, while the heading and the paragraph keep
their ids and are never touched.

## Output assembly

A `RenderedDoc` becomes a full page through one assembler, `render/page.rs`
(`assemble_html_page`). The blocks are joined into the body, and that body is dropped
into a single fixed HTML shell (the `PAGE_TEMPLATE` `format!` string) that supplies the
page chrome the reader never authored:

- A **pre-paint theme bootstrap** in `<head>` (`theme_head`): a tiny inline `<script>`
  that reads the saved theme/reader preferences and sets `data-theme` on `<html>`
  *before the first paint*, so a dark-mode reader never sees a white flash. (This is the
  one piece `--bare` output drops, because bare output is script-free; it falls back to
  CSS-only theming via a `prefers-color-scheme` media query instead.)
- The **bundled offline assets**: `base.css`, the dark layer, the optional site CSS, and
  (only when the document actually uses math) the KaTeX stylesheet, all `include_str!`d
  into the binary so a built page is self-contained with no CDN.
- A **skip-to-content link** when the body carries the focusable `<main id="tali-main">`,
  plus any `include-in-header` / `include-before-body` / `include-after-body` raw HTML.
- For a page with `{js}` cells, the vendored d3 + Observable Plot libs and the `{js}`
  enhancer (gated on the rendered body, so a page without `{js}` carries none of it).

A deck routes through `render/deck.rs` instead, and a site/book page adds chrome from
`site/`, but the **same assembler builds the page shell for the static `build` and for
both live-preview servers** (preview just injects the websocket client into that shell),
so they can't drift apart. The same block list backs all three outputs (see
[Architecture](architecture.tmd)).

## Interactive cells (`{js}`)

`{js}` cells are client-side, not kernel-executed, so they sit outside the
[execution](execution.tmd) path. A small native enhancer (`tali-js.js`)
runs each cell's source against a small per-cell scope (`tali.get`/`set`/`value`,
`onInput`, `container`, `invalidation`); it is a small scheduler, not a full
reactive VM. Cell kinds come from `//|` options: `//| viewof: name` returns a DOM
input registered under `name`, `//| name: x` publishes the return value into a
shared scope, and `//| input: a, b` re-runs the cell when input `a` or `b` fires.
From the cells' `defines`/`inputs` the enhancer builds a dependency graph
(`buildGraph`): a name → consumers map plus a global topological order via Kahn's
algorithm. When an input fires, `scheduleFrom` re-runs exactly the cell's
*transitive-downstream* closure (following each hit cell's own `defines`, so a
`n → squared → …` chain re-runs end to end), once each, in that topological order,
rather than cascading listener fires. The initial run is a single document-order
(producer-before-consumer) awaited pass; cells left over after Kahn's are in a cycle
and show a diagnostic instead of running. A Python cell's `define(...)` bridges
values across via a `<script type="tali-define">` blob the enhancer ingests,
re-running dependents when it lands after first paint.

Two decisions keep this cheap and robust. The page assembler ships the vendored
d3 + Observable Plot libs (UMD globals) **only when a page actually contains `{js}`
cells** (gated on the rendered body), so a page without them carries no extra weight.
And because every `{js}` cell is a block with a content-hash id, an unedited cell is
**never touched by the diff**, so it keeps its runtime state across a save, the same
live-state guarantee a Three.js canvas gets.
